WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.997
Redis is a program that keeps data in memory and answers requests over the network.

2
00:00:08.167 --> 00:00:15.491
Thousands of programs can talk to one Redis server at once, sending short commands like "add one to this counter".

3
00:00:15.667 --> 00:00:20.815
Redis runs all of them on a single thread, one line of work inside the program.

4
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.863
One thread, for thousands?

5
00:00:22.863 --> 00:00:24.869
That sounds slow, and risky.

6
00:00:24.869 --> 00:00:26.588
Hold on to both worries.

7
00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:30.249
The obvious design gives each client its own thread.

8
00:00:30.249 --> 00:00:35.712
A client reaches the server through a connection, an open line between two programs.

9
00:00:35.867 --> 00:00:44.471
Ten thousand clients need ten thousand threads, each with its own memory, and the computer spends much of its time switching between them.

10
00:00:44.867 --> 00:00:47.931
Worse, two threads can touch the same key at once.

11
00:00:47.931 --> 00:00:50.811
A key is a name for a value, like this counter.

12
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.702
Client A and client B both send INCR, add one.

13
00:00:54.702 --> 00:00:56.392
Both threads read 41.

14
00:00:56.392 --> 00:00:57.519
Both write 42.

15
00:00:57.519 --> 00:01:00.819
Two adds, and the counter went up by one.

16
00:01:00.967 --> 00:01:07.928
The fix is a lock on every key, a marker that lets one thread in at a time, and every command pays for it.

17
00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:13.056
What we'd like is a design where two commands can never collide, with no locks at all.

18
00:01:13.333 --> 00:01:17.031
Picture a small post office with one clerk and six windows.

19
00:01:17.233 --> 00:01:24.045
The clerk serves the first window, then the second, round the room, finishing each job before starting the next.

20
00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:28.851
There's one pair of hands, so two customers can never grab the same parcel.

21
00:01:28.851 --> 00:01:31.541
The clerk never even locks the stamp drawer.

22
00:01:31.541 --> 00:01:33.375
Who would she lock it against?

23
00:01:33.633 --> 00:01:34.484
That's Redis.

24
00:01:34.484 --> 00:01:40.239
The clerk is the one thread, the windows are the connections, and each job is a command.

25
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.230
And the clerk never waits at an empty window.

26
00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:50.461
Redis's thread asks the operating system, the program that runs the computer, which connections have bytes waiting.

27
00:01:50.667 --> 00:01:53.479
On Linux, that request is called epoll_wait.

28
00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:56.355
If none do, the thread sleeps until one does.

29
00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:59.144
Let's watch with three clients.

30
00:01:59.144 --> 00:02:00.786
Client A sends INCR.

31
00:02:00.786 --> 00:02:02.263
B asks for card:7.

32
00:02:02.263 --> 00:02:04.726
C stores a value under card:9.

33
00:02:04.726 --> 00:02:07.599
The thread is asleep, using no CPU.

34
00:02:07.767 --> 00:02:11.997
The operating system wakes it: three connections have bytes waiting.

35
00:02:12.233 --> 00:02:21.264
The thread reads A's bytes, cuts out one complete command, and runs it from start to finish: read 41, add one, store 42.

36
00:02:21.467 --> 00:02:25.495
The reply waits in A's outgoing buffer, a holding area.

37
00:02:25.495 --> 00:02:26.813
It isn't sent yet.

38
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.100
Then B's GET, then C's SET, each to the end.

39
00:02:30.367 --> 00:02:33.808
Before it sleeps again, it sends every waiting reply.

40
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.473
Ask, read, run, send.

41
00:02:35.473 --> 00:02:39.752
This loop is the event loop, and Redis runs it over and over.

42
00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:41.938
Now the race from before.

43
00:02:41.938 --> 00:02:46.055
Client A and client B both send INCR at the same moment.

44
00:02:46.055 --> 00:02:47.525
The counter says 42.

45
00:02:47.525 --> 00:02:49.069
Where does it end up?

46
00:02:49.069 --> 00:02:50.245
Pause and guess.

47
00:02:53.467 --> 00:02:53.789
44.

48
00:02:53.789 --> 00:03:01.309
One INCR runs to the end, 42 to 43, and only then the other, 43 to 44.

49
00:03:01.533 --> 00:03:09.220
Commands never overlap, so every command is atomic: it happens as one step, and no client ever sees it half done.

50
00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:15.008
Fifty programs calling INCR on one key never lose a count, with no lock at all.

51
00:03:15.008 --> 00:03:16.641
Nobody else is running.

52
00:03:16.641 --> 00:03:18.273
That's the risky worry.

53
00:03:18.533 --> 00:03:19.752
And the slow worry?

54
00:03:19.752 --> 00:03:24.051
On the lab server, a GET costs the thread about half a microsecond.

55
00:03:24.267 --> 00:03:27.122
Here the post office picture breaks, on speed.

56
00:03:27.122 --> 00:03:29.357
A clerk needs a minute per customer.

57
00:03:29.357 --> 00:03:33.951
So queues that would be hopeless at a post office stay invisible in Redis.

58
00:03:34.167 --> 00:03:36.456
Until one slow request arrives.

59
00:03:36.733 --> 00:03:41.817
Then a customer asks for four hundred parcels to be weighed, and every window waits.

60
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:48.674
In Redis, client A sends a Lua script, a small program that Redis runs as one command.

61
00:03:48.674 --> 00:03:51.080
This one spins for 1.5 seconds.

62
00:03:51.233 --> 00:03:54.664
At 300 milliseconds, B sends GET payment:42.

63
00:03:54.664 --> 00:03:59.419
The only thread that could read it is busy inside the script.

64
00:03:59.419 --> 00:04:00.043
B waits.

65
00:04:00.233 --> 00:04:10.629
At 1,500 milliseconds the script returns, and B finally gets its half a microsecond of work, 1,200 milliseconds late.

66
00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:15.201
One 1.5-second command costs as much as three million GETs.

67
00:04:15.433 --> 00:04:19.934
Somewhere in that queue, a customer with one stamp is learning patience.

68
00:04:19.934 --> 00:04:22.060
So the danger is one long command.

69
00:04:22.333 --> 00:04:23.333
Two guardrails.

70
00:04:23.333 --> 00:04:26.933
First, watch for commands whose work grows with a key.

71
00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:29.221
KEYS *, which lists every key.

72
00:04:29.221 --> 00:04:32.005
HGETALL on a hash with a million fields.

73
00:04:32.005 --> 00:04:34.023
Deleting a huge key with DEL.

74
00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:37.812
SLOWLOG GET lists every command over 10 milliseconds.

75
00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:41.243
Second, atomic covers one command, not three.

76
00:04:41.467 --> 00:04:46.892
A program with eight threads counts payments: GET the counter, add one, SET it back.

77
00:04:47.067 --> 00:04:51.340
After 8,000 calls it printed 1,611.

78
00:04:51.533 --> 00:04:55.529
Between one GET and its SET, the loop ran other clients' commands.

79
00:04:55.733 --> 00:05:02.353
Send INCR instead, and the read, the add and the write are one command: 8,000, every run.

80
00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:04.394
So the moral of the story.

81
00:05:04.394 --> 00:05:07.065
One thread runs every command to the end.

82
00:05:07.233 --> 00:05:12.701
Each command is atomic, with no locks, and one slow command makes everyone wait.

83
00:05:12.933 --> 00:05:14.167
One quick check.

84
00:05:14.167 --> 00:05:20.721
A script runs for two seconds, and C asks for card:9, a key the script never touches.

85
00:05:20.721 --> 00:05:21.646
Does C wait?

86
00:05:21.646 --> 00:05:22.880
Pause and think.

87
00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:26.437
Yes.

88
00:05:26.437 --> 00:05:30.487
One job at a time, whatever parcel you came for.
